course: dictionary
lesson: 6365
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and mu is one of them. It can be a Greek letter, a symbol in science, a sound in Mandarin, or a word in Japanese Zen. You cannot tell which one is meant from the word alone, so you must look at the words around it.
explain: «In Kel, pa = 1, ki = 2, ro = 3, mu = 4 and lan = 5.» The sentence is a list, and it says mu = 4. Here the clues are the equals signs, so mu must be a number, and it is 4.
explain: «In cartoons, a cow says moo, not mu.» The word not tells you that mu and moo are different. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Mu is the twelfth letter of the Greek alphabet, the Greek m. Its symbol is μ.
fact: μ stands for micro-, which means one millionth, so a μm (micrometre) is a millionth of a metre.
fact: In Mandarin, mù with a falling tone means tree or wood (木). Tones change the meaning of a sound.
fact: In Japanese Zen, mu can mean nothing or not having, and it is not an ordinary English word.
exercise: In «a μm is a millionth of a metre», what does μ stand for? | kilo-, a thousand times bigger | mega-, a million times bigger | milli-, one thousandth | *micro-, one millionth
exercise: «In cartoons, a cow says moo, not mu.» What sound does a cow make in cartoons? | Oink | *Moo | Mu | Baa
exercise: Which letter is the Greek m? | *mu | pi | nu | tau
test: In Kel, ro = 3 and lan = 5, and «a number before lan multiplies it». What is ro lan? | 35 | 5 | *15 | 8
test: In Mandarin, mù (木) with a falling tone means what? | A cow | The Greek letter | *Tree or wood | Nothing at all
exam: In Japanese Zen, mu can mean what? | A loud noise | A tree | *Nothing or not having | A million parts
